{"term":"契兄弟","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"qì xiōngdì","category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"male homoerotic bond","concept":"sworn_homoerotic_brotherhood","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"archaic","crude":10,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":35,"recognition_pct_peak":55,"peak_year":null,"regional":"CN-FJ","definition":"Historical South Chinese institution of male sworn brotherhood that functioned as a formalized same-sex domestic and romantic partnership, documented primarily in Fujian and Guangdong.","english_term":null,"english_translation_quality":"none","notes":"Distinct from ordinary sworn brotherhood (结拜兄弟) by its romantic and sometimes sexual dimensions; participants cohabited and maintained lifelong bonds akin to marriage."}
{"term":"金兰契","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"jīnlán qì","category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"female homoerotic bond","concept":"sworn_homoerotic_sisterhood","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"archaic","crude":8,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":28,"recognition_pct_peak":42,"peak_year":null,"regional":"CN-GD","definition":"Historical Cantonese institution of female sworn sisterhood in which women pledged celibacy from men and formed exclusive domestic partnerships, sometimes with erotic dimensions; literally 'golden-orchid covenant.'","english_term":null,"english_translation_quality":"none","notes":"Prevalent in the Pearl River Delta silk industry where economically independent women could sustain such partnerships; closely related to the 自梳女 (self-combing women) tradition; the 金兰 phrase derives from the I Ching metaphor for two hearts in sympathy."}
{"term":"磨镜","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"mó jìng","category":"acts","subcategory":"tribadism","concept":"tribadism","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":40,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":28,"recognition_pct_peak":38,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Classical Chinese euphemism for tribadism or vulva-to-vulva sexual contact between women; literally 'polishing/grinding mirrors,' with the mirror as a conventional metaphor for the vulva.","english_term":"tribadism","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Found in Qing dynasty erotic fiction including 品花宝鉴; the mirror metaphor derives from the vulva's symmetrical, reflective shape; the term is entirely archaic and would confuse most modern speakers without context."}
{"term":"太监","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"tàijiān","category":"third_gender_roles","subcategory":"eunuch","concept":"imperial_eunuch","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"archaic","crude":8,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":97,"recognition_pct_peak":97,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A castrated male servant of the Chinese imperial court; eunuchs occupied a distinct social and gender category with privileged access to the emperor and imperial family's private quarters.","english_term":"eunuch","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The institution ended with the fall of the Qing dynasty in 1912; universally recognized through compulsory history education and period dramas; the last imperial eunuch, Sun Yaoting, died in 1996; differs from 'eunuch' in implying a specific imperial bureaucratic role."}
{"term":"宦官","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"huànguān","category":"third_gender_roles","subcategory":"eunuch","concept":"imperial_eunuch","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"classical_specialized","crude":5,"technical":45,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Classical and formal Mandarin term for a castrated male official serving in the imperial court; more literary and bureaucratic in register than the colloquial 太监.","english_term":"eunuch","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Used in formal histories and classical texts; 宦 originally referred to court service generally, not castration; the castration requirement became standard during the Han dynasty; less familiar to non-scholarly speakers than 太监."}
{"term":"人妖","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"rényāo","category":"third_gender_roles","subcategory":"transgender woman slur","concept":"ladyboy","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":65,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":95,"recognition_pct_peak":95,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Pejorative or colloquial Mandarin term for a transgender woman or male-to-female gender-nonconforming person, especially a performer; literally 'human demon/monster.'","english_term":"ladyboy","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Widely applied to Thai kathoey performers in Chinese media; carries strong stigmatizing connotation in mainland PRC; partially reclaimed in entertainment and nightlife contexts; offensiveness is roughly parallel to English 'tranny.'"}
{"term":"伪娘","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"wèiniáng","category":"third_gender_roles","subcategory":"femboy","concept":"cross_dressing","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"subculture","crude":18,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":74,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":2015,"regional":null,"definition":"Internet and anime subculture term for a biological male who presents convincingly as female through dress and mannerism; literally 'fake girl/woman'; analogous to 'femboy' in Anglophone internet culture.","english_term":"femboy","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Popularized through anime fandoms and Chinese BBS culture circa 2010–2018; generally neutral to appreciative in online communities; culturally distinct from 人妖 in implying voluntary aesthetic performance rather than transgender identity; recognition declining as younger users prefer newer terms."}
{"term":"假小子","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"jiǎ xiǎozi","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"tomboy","concept":"butch_identity","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":5,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":93,"recognition_pct_peak":93,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Common Mandarin colloquial term for a tomboyish girl or young woman who adopts masculine dress, behavior, or interests; literally 'fake boy/lad'; does not inherently imply lesbian identity.","english_term":"tomboy","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Generally neutral to affectionate in tone; lacks the explicit lesbian connotation 'butch' carries in English; frequently applied to sporty or adventurous girls in casual speech and fiction."}
{"term":"男人婆","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"nánrén pó","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"butch woman","concept":"butch_identity","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":22,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Mandarin colloquial term for a masculine-presenting or mannish adult woman; carries mild pejorative connotation; literally 'man-woman'; applied to adults rather than girls.","english_term":"mannish woman","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Slightly more pejorative than 假小子; implies a grown woman who fails to conform to feminine gender expectations; may or may not imply lesbian identity depending on context; 婆 (old woman) adds a slightly contemptuous edge absent in 假小子."}
{"term":"中性人","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"zhōngxìng rén","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"androgynous person","concept":"non_binary","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":5,"technical":25,"recognition_pct_current":62,"recognition_pct_peak":68,"peak_year":2018,"regional":null,"definition":"Mandarin term for a gender-neutral or androgynous person; in Chinese usage often describes people with androgynous physical appearance rather than necessarily claiming a non-binary gender identity in the Western sense.","english_term":"non-binary","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"中性 literally means 'neutral/neuter'; the term gained currency with K-pop and Chinese androgynous male idol aesthetics (小鲜肉 era); the Western non-binary identity framework does not map cleanly onto Chinese 中性 usage, which is more aesthetic than political."}
{"term":"双性人","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"shuāngxìng rén","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"intersex","concept":"intersex","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":8,"technical":55,"recognition_pct_current":55,"recognition_pct_peak":55,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Mandarin term for an intersex person; a person born with sex characteristics that do not fit typical binary male or female definitions; literally 'dual-sex person.'","english_term":"intersex","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"More colloquial than the medical term 间性人 (jiānxìng rén); 双性 also means 'bisexual' in some compounds (双性恋), so context is required to distinguish intersex from bisexual meaning."}
{"term":"青楼","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"qīnglóu","category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"brothel","concept":"brothel","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":18,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":85,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Classical Chinese literary term for a pleasure house or upscale brothel where courtesans entertained clients with music, poetry, and sexual services; literally 'blue/green tower.'","english_term":"brothel","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The 'blue/green' color originally referred to painted roof tiles of entertainers' residences; the term evokes the cultivated, aestheticized nature of Tang-Song dynasty courtesan culture and is far more literary and less crude than direct words for brothel; widely recognized through classical poetry and period dramas."}
{"term":"花街柳巷","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"huā jiē liǔ xiàng","category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"red light district","concept":"red_light_district","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":15,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":68,"recognition_pct_peak":73,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Classical Chinese four-character idiom for the entertainment and prostitution quarter; literally 'flower streets and willow alleys'; both flower and willow are conventional classical poetic symbols for courtesans.","english_term":"red light district","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"A chengyu (成语); 花 and 柳 are classical erotic metaphors making this highly euphemistic; still appears in modern literary writing and journalism as a literary shorthand for red-light areas."}
{"term":"烟花女子","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"yānhuā nǚzǐ","category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"sex worker archaic","concept":"sex_worker_archaic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":12,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":58,"recognition_pct_peak":63,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Classical Chinese literary euphemism for a prostitute or courtesan; 烟花 (fireworks/smoke and flowers) is a classical metaphor evoking fleeting beauty and the transient pleasure-quarter world.","english_term":"courtesan","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Encodes both the allure and transience of the courtesan's life through the fireworks/smoke metaphor; found throughout classical poetry and vernacular fiction; the 烟花 element also evokes the colloquial 烟花场所 (entertainment venues) in modern regulatory language."}
{"term":"风尘女子","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"fēngchén nǚzǐ","category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"sex worker archaic","concept":"sex_worker_archaic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":12,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":65,"recognition_pct_peak":68,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literary Chinese euphemism for a woman living outside respectable society through sex work or social disgrace; literally 'wind and dust woman,' evoking itinerancy and moral marginalization.","english_term":"fallen woman","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"风尘 (wind and dust) evokes the nomadic, exposed existence of the social margins; used in contemporary literary writing to describe both historical and modern sex workers with distancing sympathy; less specific than 妓女 and more implicitly compassionate in tone."}
{"term":"勾栏","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"gōulán","category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"entertainment venue","concept":"brothel","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":12,"technical":30,"recognition_pct_current":40,"recognition_pct_peak":48,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Song and Yuan dynasty enclosed entertainment venue housing theatrical performances, acrobatics, storytelling, and associated sex work; literally 'hooked railing,' referring to the fence enclosing the performance area.","english_term":"pleasure house","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Primarily an entertainment complex rather than a dedicated brothel; sex work was embedded in the broader entertainment economy; the concept sits between 'theater' and 'brothel' with no precise English equivalent; contained within the larger 瓦子 entertainment district."}
{"term":"瓦子","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"wǎzi","category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"entertainment district","concept":"red_light_district","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":8,"technical":30,"recognition_pct_current":33,"recognition_pct_peak":40,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Song dynasty commercial entertainment district containing theaters, marketplaces, storytellers, and entertainment venues including sex work; literally 'tile market'; a precursor to modern red-light and entertainment districts.","english_term":"pleasure quarter","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"More general than a red-light district — encompassed legitimate commerce and performance alongside sex work; 勾栏 theaters were located within 瓦子; term is primarily of historical interest, known mainly to students of Song dynasty history and classical literature."}
{"term":"歌妓","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"gējì","category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"courtesan","concept":"courtesan","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":20,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":58,"recognition_pct_peak":63,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Classical Chinese term for a female singing entertainer who also provided social and often sexual companionship to patrons; a singing courtesan skilled in music, poetry, and conversation.","english_term":"singing courtesan","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"妓 (jì) in classical Chinese meant 'entertainer,' not strictly 'prostitute'; semantic narrowing to 'prostitute' is a modern development; 歌妓 were analogous to Japanese geisha in providing musical and social companionship, though sexual services were common; frequent figures in Tang and Song dynasty poetry."}
{"term":"舞妓","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"wǔjì","category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"courtesan","concept":"courtesan","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":20,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":48,"recognition_pct_peak":53,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Classical Chinese term for a female dancing entertainer who provided social and often sexual companionship to patrons; a dancing courtesan skilled in performance dance.","english_term":"dancing courtesan","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Parallel to 歌妓 with dance as the primary skill rather than song; both terms appear as emblems of pleasure-quarter culture in Tang and Song dynasty poetry; less recognized by modern speakers than 歌妓."}
{"term":"官妓","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"guānjì","category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"state courtesan","concept":"courtesan","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":25,"technical":25,"recognition_pct_current":40,"recognition_pct_peak":48,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Imperial Chinese term for a state-registered courtesan required to serve at official government banquets and entertainments; literally 'government courtesan'; legally distinct from private prostitutes.","english_term":"state courtesan","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"官妓 were registered with and supervised by local governments; required to entertain officials at public functions without fee, permitted to accept private clients otherwise; the institution was formally abolished during the Ming dynasty but persisted informally; the public/private distinction had legal and tax implications."}
{"term":"私妓","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"sījì","category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"unlicensed sex worker","concept":"sex_worker","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":42,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":35,"recognition_pct_peak":43,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Imperial Chinese term for a private or unlicensed prostitute operating outside the official courtesan registry; literally 'private prostitute'; contrasted with the state-registered 官妓.","english_term":"unlicensed prostitute","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The official/private distinction carried legal and fiscal implications; 私妓 operated without sanction and were subject to periodic crackdowns; the term is largely unknown outside specialist historians of imperial Chinese social history."}
{"term":"扬州瘦马","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"Yángzhōu shòumǎ","category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"groomed concubine commodity","concept":"groomed_concubine_commodity","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"archaic","crude":20,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":48,"recognition_pct_peak":55,"peak_year":null,"regional":"CN-JS","definition":"Ming and Qing dynasty term for young women purchased from poor Yangzhou-area families, intensively trained in refined arts and etiquette, and sold at high prices to wealthy men as concubines or high-class courtesans; literally 'Yangzhou thin horse.'","english_term":null,"english_translation_quality":"none","notes":"The 'thin horse' metaphor evokes the lean, elegant physique cultivated through strict diet and physical training; documented in detail by Zhang Dai's 陶庵梦忆; represents a commodified grooming pipeline from poverty to concubinage with no precise English equivalent; distinct from both sex trafficking and ordinary concubinage."}
{"term":"粉头","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"fěntóu","category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"sex worker pejorative","concept":"promiscuous_woman_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":58,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":26,"recognition_pct_peak":48,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Pejorative historical slang for a heavily made-up woman of easy virtue or a prostitute; literally 'powder head,' with thick cosmetics as a classical marker of the courtesan or promiscuous woman.","english_term":"painted woman","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The powder/cosmetics association links to the classical trope of heavy makeup as a signal of sexual availability; largely archaic in current use, surviving mainly in historical fiction and period drama; would confuse most speakers under 50 without context."}
{"term":"花魁","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"huākuí","category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"top courtesan","concept":"courtesan","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":15,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":65,"recognition_pct_peak":70,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The most acclaimed and highest-ranking courtesan in a brothel or pleasure district, selected by reputation, artistic skill, and beauty; literally 'flower champion/leader'; analogous to the Japanese oiran.","english_term":"prima courtesan","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"花魁 were celebrated cultural figures attracting literary men and wealthy patrons; appears in famous classical stories including 卖油郎独占花魁; sits between 'courtesan' and 'celebrity sex worker' with no single English equivalent; recognized through classical literature education and period drama."}
{"term":"龟公","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"guīgōng","category":"sex_work_pornography","subcategory":"pimp","concept":"pimp","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":82,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":68,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Crude Mandarin slang for a pimp or the husband who profits from his wife's prostitution; the most dishonored male role in the traditional Chinese honor-shame economy; literally 'turtle duke/gentleman.'","english_term":"pimp","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The turtle (龟/王八) carries exceptional cultural weight in Chinese insult culture, uniquely combining cuckoldry, cowardice, and moral degradation in one symbol; 龟公 implies both the pimping function and the dishonor of tolerating and profiting from one's wife's sex work; related compound 王八蛋 ('turtle egg') is among the most common Chinese expletives."}
